00:45:33,333 --> 00:45:39,333
Jiro Dreams of Sushi is a 2011 Japanese-language American
documentary film directed by David Gelb.

11
00:50:06,666 --> 00:50:12,666
The film follows Jiro Ono (小野 二郎, Ono Jirō), a then-85-year-old sushi master and
owner of Sukiyabashi Jiro, then a Michelin three-star restaurant.

12
00:54:40,000 --> 00:54:46,000
Sukiyabashi Jiro is a 10-seat, sushi-only restaurant
located in a Tokyo subway station.

13
00:59:13,333 --> 00:59:19,333
As of 2026, Jiro Ono serves a tasting menu of roughly 20
courses, for a minimum of JP¥88,000 (US$557).

14
01:03:46,666 --> 01:03:52,666
The film also profiles Jiro's two sons, both of whom are also sushi chefs.

15
01:08:20,000 --> 01:08:26,000
The younger son, Takashi (隆士), left Sukiyabashi Jiro to open a mirror
image of his father's restaurant in Roppongi Hills.

16
01:12:53,333 --> 01:12:59,333
The 50-year-old elder son, Yoshikazu (禎一), obliged to succeed his father, still works for Jiro
and is faced with the prospect of one day taking over the flagship restaurant.

17
01:17:26,666 --> 01:17:32,666
The film explores themes of japan, restaurant, sushi
restaurant, apprentice, based on true story.
